            {
                "entry_id": "6ea96c02-ccb6-3483-a956-4c5f7500d446",
                "word": "anxiogène",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "4c5e6b19-7970-4bc8-a858-24cf64166aef",
                        "entry_id": "6ea96c02-ccb6-3483-a956-4c5f7500d446",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "anxiogènes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "e58dd848-ec37-3d7f-8404-4e1b98d4b0dc",
                        "entry_id": "6ea96c02-ccb6-3483-a956-4c5f7500d446",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Qui suscite l’anxiété, l’angoisse, l’inquiétude."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "6f065e31-33dd-4ac1-96a1-0a2ecccd3498",
                                "sense_id": "e58dd848-ec37-3d7f-8404-4e1b98d4b0dc",
                                "text": "Aussi l’enregistrement graphique des résultats de son ingénieuse méthode vient-il scientifiquement confirmer, par une sorte de contre-épreuve, l’influence anxiogène, constatée par nous, des associations libres, c’est-à-dire de la déconcentration."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "738b328d-7ab9-4941-bd93-04811151ad6f",
                                "sense_id": "e58dd848-ec37-3d7f-8404-4e1b98d4b0dc",
                                "text": "« Ainsi, entre novembre 2012 et janvier 2013, 162 piétons sont décédés, soit en trois mois, 34,1 % de la mortalité routière “piétonne” annuelle », relève l’Observatoire interministériel de la sécurité routière. Qui chaque année, y va de son communiqué de presse un brin anxiogène, intitulé l’année dernière : « Heure d’hiver, piétons, attention ! »"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4abd777a-f568-46c0-ab32-2adf231ef3c7",
                                "sense_id": "e58dd848-ec37-3d7f-8404-4e1b98d4b0dc",
                                "text": "À force de vivre enfermés dans un environnement anxiogène, ils deviennent fous."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "009bcd57-fd37-4718-b6d2-154d38903611",
                                "sense_id": "e58dd848-ec37-3d7f-8404-4e1b98d4b0dc",
                                "text": "Les enfants portaient tout au long de la journée des badges de verre afin de mesurer la dose de radiation externe cumulée reçue par chacun. Les données n’étaient pas visibles par les intéressés, qui devaient les rendre au bout de quatre mois aux autorités compétentes. Une situation particulièrement anxiogène."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "fd98ed28-da3a-49a3-a1b6-46cfe317e2db",
                        "source_entry_id": "6ea96c02-ccb6-3483-a956-4c5f7500d446",
                        "source_sense_id": "e58dd848-ec37-3d7f-8404-4e1b98d4b0dc",
                        "target_lang_word": "worrisome",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "worrisome",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "21c07da1-4d21-3c2b-8684-afe6ad396f6a",
                                        "entry_id": "c249c914-cead-3d8a-a2e2-6a8f07844580",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Causing worry; perturbing or vexing."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ed53e436-9aee-4eb4-951e-4273624099df",
                                                "sense_id": "21c07da1-4d21-3c2b-8684-afe6ad396f6a",
                                                "text": "He ’s got a cow, too; she ’s got the worrisomest horns ever was. I believe she ’s a steer anyway."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "aca6f519-7204-434a-a44a-57e134bda3ad",
                                                "sense_id": "21c07da1-4d21-3c2b-8684-afe6ad396f6a",
                                                "text": "OUR “WORRISOMEST” WEEDS / Johnson grass and Bermuda grass will be growing fast by late May."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7ef78a06-0cc7-4fcc-a878-e042f8c90e4d",
                                                "sense_id": "21c07da1-4d21-3c2b-8684-afe6ad396f6a",
                                                "text": "“[…] Just supposing Emily poisoned him, why would she have done it? What could she have against a four-year-old?” / Star sighed. “Mr. Hacey, you do ask me the worrisomest questions. How I know why somebody who maybe didn’t even poison somebody come to poison that somebody?”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "55a56717-a70d-450e-a1a0-611ba57717a4",
                                                "sense_id": "21c07da1-4d21-3c2b-8684-afe6ad396f6a",
                                                "text": "“Doesn't everyone call his doctor every week or two for reassurance about some worrisome symptom?”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "50133f16-6163-4041-87bb-53c247b8fe5d",
                                                "sense_id": "21c07da1-4d21-3c2b-8684-afe6ad396f6a",
                                                "text": "“How can you be sure you won’t change your mind?” / For god’s sake, can’t he take a hint? / Maybe you’re not convincing enough? Because … you don’t want to be? / “Because I’m actively working on not changing my mind,” I say in order to drown out my evil inner voices. / “What if I’m actively working on swaying it?” / “Ugh! Stop it,” I say, but I can’t help but laugh. God, I think I like arguing with him. That’s very worrisome. Very, very worrisome. / It gets worrisomer when he leans towards me and asks very quietly and calmly, “Why?”"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4d8056d4-8277-4a67-aaf9-f0b11a356f67",
                                                "sense_id": "21c07da1-4d21-3c2b-8684-afe6ad396f6a",
                                                "text": "There's more to The Morning Joe/Donald Trump spat than was apparent when the U.S. president launched his Twitter attack at the show's hosts on Thursday. New details add context to the story that make it both curiouser and curiouser and muckier and muckier — not to mention worrisomer."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "2b931049-8346-3fe1-b60d-2f6ce8210a7e",
                                        "entry_id": "c249c914-cead-3d8a-a2e2-6a8f07844580",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Inclined to worry."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "of a person"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "84e125ad-01be-418d-822d-da3e422caf95",
                                                "sense_id": "2b931049-8346-3fe1-b60d-2f6ce8210a7e",
                                                "text": "I use to would perform that song when I was singin in Chance’s band. We had a real sweet, dreamy, Dinah Washington-kinda arrangement of it that’d make even the worrisomest drunk pipe down and listen."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "f5026c81-8ec0-4a37-bf2c-d24d74bbace2",
                        "source_entry_id": "6ea96c02-ccb6-3483-a956-4c5f7500d446",
                        "source_sense_id": "e58dd848-ec37-3d7f-8404-4e1b98d4b0dc",
                        "target_lang_word": "anxiogenic",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "anxiogenic",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "b12cbd13-f33f-312c-98d3-f151a4a861d5",
                                        "entry_id": "0ecbac20-0784-38be-a640-e37cfe471bf3",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Causing anxiety, or pertaining to the causing of anxiety."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "987106c0-57bf-47fd-942f-4d332e2ba4a6",
                                                "sense_id": "b12cbd13-f33f-312c-98d3-f151a4a861d5",
                                                "text": "A CRF antagonist, -helical CRF(9-41), can also attenuate the anxiogenic behavioral effects of HU-210."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "01151f4e-6397-4162-875e-8acc67530873",
                                                "sense_id": "b12cbd13-f33f-312c-98d3-f151a4a861d5",
                                                "text": "Increases in dopamine efflux in the NAS cannot simply be seen as equivalent to drug reward, because aversive stimuli, such as foot shock, and anxiogenic drugs, such as FG 7142 and beta-CCE (which are not self-administered), have exactly the same neurochemical effect (3 )."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "92602a14-4f10-43cf-901d-3b2aabf66d7a",
                                                "sense_id": "b12cbd13-f33f-312c-98d3-f151a4a861d5",
                                                "text": "Central injection of substance P or related peptide agonists induces conditioned place aversion and produces an anxiogenic profile on the elevated plus maze, implying that activation of central substance P pathways is aversive (5 )."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "634fc66f-f650-4b4e-a0fd-1dd21955ac9d",
                                                "sense_id": "b12cbd13-f33f-312c-98d3-f151a4a861d5",
                                                "text": "Thus, self-directed behavior increases after anxiogenic drug treatment but decreases after anxiolytic drug treatment (29 )."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e2cadad8-3077-4051-ac75-cb68825b89af",
                                                "sense_id": "b12cbd13-f33f-312c-98d3-f151a4a861d5",
                                                "text": "This piece will perform a micro-excavation of these toplayers of the literary soil to suggest anxiogenic literature has the potential not only to adumbrate the post-apocalypse, a common theme in contemporary literature, but also to anticipate the post-Anthropocene."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "anxiogenic",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "027269fc-f45c-3580-aa26-f0ded3a033be",
                                        "entry_id": "ed02536f-46ae-338f-b967-a62739b9b4e4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "An anxiogenic drug: a drug that causes anxiety."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
